FINDINGS FROM THE EDA SCRIPTS


- Broken rows:
	there are 3 movies, each split over two rows. The overview contains a newline:
	the first half of the row stops in the middle of overview.
	The movies are Midnight Man, Mardock Scramble and Avalanche Sharks. Without these rows,
	45,460 valid rows remain.

	They can easily be rebuilt

- Duplicated IDs:
	29, for a total of 59 rows.
	28 IDs appear 2 times while one ID appears 3 times
	16 IDs have identical copies, for a total of 33 rows (keeping one copy is enough)
	11 IDs differ only in popularity
	2 differ in popularity and vote_count (the higher vote count is kept, as it is the most recent)
	In the 2 cases we can verify, i.e. those with a different vote_count, the copy that appears further down in the file is the most up to date. This suggests that the rows at the end were downloaded later.

	For the 11 copies with only a different popularity, the proposal is therefore to keep the last occurrence in the file. The advantages:
	- it is a single rule that works for all three cases: nothing changes for identical copies, and for those with a different vote_count it matches the previous rule;
	- it is deterministic and easy to explain in the report;
	- in code it is one line: drop_duplicates("id", keep="last").

- Missing values: budget 80%, revenue 84%, homepage 83%, tagline 55%, empty production_companies 26%.
	The runtime is missing or 0 in 1,815 movies, i.e. 4%

- No problems with types, imdb_id format, language codes and nested fields: they can all be read and every
	id always corresponds to the same name.

- 59 languages have an empty name
	this means there are 59 language codes that NEVER have an associated name. It is not a problem because the language can be rebuilt from the code, and queries on languages should be done on the code. Nevertheless, the missing field can easily be rebuilt.

	out of a total of 133 languages, 74 always have the name and 59 never have it

- Huge runtimes: 16 movies last more than 600 minutes. They are TV series, for example Centennial with 1,256
	minutes.


- Strange dates: 4 movies are earlier than 1888, but they are correct: they are the very first films in
	history. 31 movies have a date after the moment the dataset was downloaded.

- Placeholder overviews: "No overview found." appears in 133 movies, plus variants such as "No Overview".
	Consequences
	- During cleaning they must be turned into missing values, like the empty fields.
	- If they are not cleaned, a text search on the overview finds them. For example, searching for the word "found" returns 133 unrelated movies.

	Limitation of the check
	It only finds placeholders repeated at least twice. A variant written in a unique way, for example "Overview not available", would appear in a single movie and the script would not see it. Such cases are probably rare, but it is not guaranteed.


